    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.data4thepeople.com/p/income-health-viz#faq",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is income linked to health in the United States?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In this data, closely. Across 77,708 census tracts, the rank correlation between median household income and a health index built from 14 CDC measures is 0.82. Part of that link is built into CDC's estimates, which use poverty as an input. At the county level, income also tracks life expectancy from death records, at 0.73."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does health keep improving at higher incomes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, in this data, though more slowly. Above about $110,000 in median household income, the health index rises about 27% as fast for each added dollar as it does below that level. It still rises in each of the 45 states with enough tracts to test."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is life expectancy linked to income by county?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, closely. Life expectancy comes from death records, not a model, so it is the cleanest check in this chart. Split U.S. counties into 20 equal-size groups by median household income, and the middle county's life expectancy runs from 70.7 years in the lowest-income group to 80.8 years in the highest. The rank correlation is 0.73. A 2016 study that matched tax records to death records for millions of Americans found that life expectancy rises with income at every income level."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which counties have the lowest life expectancy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Among the counties in this chart, the five lowest, for 2021 to 2023, are all in South Dakota: Buffalo County (52.9 years), Dewey County (57.3), Oglala Lakota County (58.4), Corson County (58.5) and Todd County (58.5). All five are small, so each figure has a wide range around it; Buffalo County's published range runs from 48.8 to 57.1 years."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is income linked to obesity and diabetes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In CDC's estimates, yes. Split census tracts into 20 equal-size groups by median household income. In the lowest-income group (median income about $31,000), the middle tract has 45.8% of adults with obesity and 20.2% with diabetes. In the highest-income group (about $197,000), it is 23.6% and 8.6%. These are model estimates built partly from poverty, so part of the link is built in."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a census tract?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A small area the Census Bureau draws to hold about 4,000 people. Tracts are often used as a stand-in for neighborhoods."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where does the health data come from?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "From CDC's PLACES project, 2025 release. CDC uses a national health survey, each area's population makeup and its poverty level to estimate how common each condition is in every tract and county. The numbers are estimates, not counts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why are Kentucky and Pennsylvania missing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "CDC's 2025 PLACES release has no estimates for either state for the measures based on its 2023 survey, which covers 13 of the 14 measures in the health index."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I see my county?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Switch Show to Counties, pick your state in the Area menu, and hover over the dots. In the tract view, each tract's box names its county."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does higher income cause better health?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "This chart does not test that. It shows which places have both higher incomes and better health estimates. Many other things, such as age, access to care and the CDC model itself, may sit behind the pattern."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the chart free to use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. It is free to use and needs no sign-in."
          }
        }
      ]
